'## Provenance and publication history\n\nThe archaeological context most 
clearly documented in the supplied material is the Palace of Nestor at Ano 
Englianos in Messenia, not the Ring of Nestor itself. Excavations begun in 1939
uncovered the palace, and the excavation project’s later publications include 
works on the history of investigations in Messenia, the discovery of the 
palace, and the palace’s Linear B documents.[1][2] The palace excavation was 
conducted in annual campaigns between 1952 and 1964, and the site was destroyed
by fire at the end of the 13th century BCE.[3] None of these records supplies a
findspot, accession number, ownership history, or custody chain for an artifact
specifically identified as the Ring of Nestor.[1][3]\n\nThe distinction matters
because the supplied Pylos material also discusses the Griffin Warrior burial 
and other prestigious objects. A 2025 publication associated with exhibitions 
in Kalamata, Malibu, and Athens documents the wider Pylos project, but the 
supplied catalogue description does not state that the Ring of Nestor was 
exhibited or identify its owner or custodian.[4] Likewise, a report on a 2015 
burial near the Palace of Nestor labels a photographed object a signet ring 
from the Warrior Tomb, but it does not call that object the Ring of Nestor.[5] 
The available evidence therefore does not support a direct provenance claim 
linking the named ring to the palace, the Griffin Warrior burial, or the 
Messenian exhibitions.\n\nThe publication record is also narrower than a 
provenance record. The Pylos Regional Archaeological Project identifies 
Hesperia as a principal venue for publishing project results and lists books 
and chapters on Pylos, Messenia, and the Palace of Nestor, but the supplied 
entries do not reproduce an object catalogue for the ring.[2] One description 
states that Linear B tablets 1–1589 are presented, while the contents list 
labels Part 1 as Documents 1–616 and Part 2 as Documents 622–1589; the supplied
page does not explain the apparent gap for Documents 617–621.[6] That numbering
issue concerns the publication of tablets, not the ring, and cannot be used to 
infer a provenance history.\n\nA separate study of the Thessaloniki museum 
object MΘ 23748 reports a different provenance problem: a claim that the ring 
was found in a Thessaloniki grave in 1867 is described as vague, unverified, or
incorrect, and the object was delivered to the Archaeological Museum of 
Thessaloniki in 2005.[7] The study does not associate MΘ 23748 with Pylos or 
with the name Ring of Nestor. The supplied sources thus describe potentially 
different entities, and no secure chain of discovery, ownership, custody, and 
publication can be reconstructed for the Ring of Nestor itself.', 
