commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-01ca8ecd', 'content': '**Ring of Nestor** is a name used for an Aegean gold
ring whose imagery and authenticity have been debated in archaeological 
literature. A 2011 peer-reviewed article states that the ring was reportedly 
found in 1924, that Sir Arthur Evans accepted it as authentic, and that other 
scholars judged it a forgery; the same abstract reports later renewed claims of
authenticity.[5] Theodore Eliopoulos published a 2013 study specifically on the
ring’s iconography.[1]\n\nThe terminology is not uniform. An archival study 
uses “Minos Ring,” while one of its references uses “Pseudo-Minoan Nestor 
Ring”; the cited text does not explain whether those designations refer to the 
same object.[8] The ring is also distinct in the cited publications from the 
gold signet rings reported from the Griffin Warrior burial at Pylos, which are 
not identified as the Ring of Nestor.[15]\n\nThe authenticity question has 
competing reported positions. Marinatos and Jackson associate the alleged 
discovery circumstances and the butterfly motif with an argument against 
authenticity.[5] A separate archival-study abstract says that Spyridon 
Marinatos and Georg Karo regarded the “Minos Ring” as a forgery, while evidence
in an appendix was said to suggest genuineness.[8] These accounts differ both 
in their authenticity assessments and in their terminology; because the cited 
publications do not conclusively equate all the names, the positions are 
presented as a documented disagreement rather than a resolved verdict.', 
'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e41', 'e42', 'e43', 'e44', 'e45', 'e46', 'e56', 'e57',
'e58', 'e59', 'e28', 'e29', 'e31', 'e32'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1', 
'x2', 'x3']}, {'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 'b2-526c2c41', 'content': '## 
Description and iconography\n\nA 2013 article by Theodore Eliopoulos is 
specifically devoted to the iconography of the “Ring of Nestor.”[1] A separate 
study of Thessaloniki museum inventory MΘ 23748 describes an unknown-provenance
gold signet ring with an elongated, almost wedge-shaped bezel, a flat-convex 
upper surface, a concave reverse, and a nearly circular plain hoop.[16] Its 
intaglio depicts two horned, hoofed quadrupeds in a mating scene, although 
their species cannot be identified securely.[16] That study does not equate MΘ 
23748 with the Ring of Nestor; the physical description therefore remains 
attached to that inventory number.[16]\n\nSeparate Pylos publications describe 
gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior burial. One displayed summary 
describes a ring with a seascape, a shrine flanked by palm trees, and five 
female figures; the summary does not call it the Ring of Nestor.[15] The cited 
Pylos material therefore distinguishes those Pylos rings from the ring 
discussed in the 2011 Nestor Ring study.[5][15]\n\nNanno Marinatos, an 
archaeologist and co-author with Briana Jackson of the 2011 peer-reviewed 
study, presents semantic coherence and Egyptian prototypes as part of the 
study’s interpretive framework; its abstract states that the butterfly motif 
argues against authenticity.[5] The cited archival study uses the name “Minos 
Ring,” while a reference title uses “Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring.”[8] This naming
difference is reported without treating the designations as proven synonyms.', 
'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e5', 'e6', 'e15', 'e16', 'e25', 'e31', 'e32', 'e34', 
'e36', 'e37', 'e38', 'e41', 'e44', 'e45', 'e46', 'e56', 'e59', 'e61'], 
'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1', 'x2', 'x3']}, {'chapter_id': 3, 
'bundle_token': 'b3-e962daf5', 'content': '## Provenance and publication 
history\n\nThe documented archaeological setting associated with the name 
Nestor is the Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos in Messenia. Excavations begun 
in 1939 uncovered the palace, and subsequent project publications address the 
history of investigations in Messenia, the palace, and its Linear B 
documents.[10][9][20] The palace excavation continued in annual campaigns from 
1952 to 1964, while the palace itself was destroyed by fire at the end of the 
13th century BCE.[19] These facts establish the context of Pylos archaeology, 
not the findspot or date of the Ring of Nestor.[9][10][19]\n\nA 2025 
publication describes exhibitions in Kalamata, Malibu, and Athens devoted to 
the wider Kingdom of Pylos, but the catalogue description does not identify the
Ring of Nestor as an exhibited object or provide its ownership and custody 
history.[10] The cited Pylos publications likewise do not provide an 
object-level discovery or accession record for the named ring.[9][10]\n\nThe 
available publication listings therefore do not establish a continuous 
chronology of discovery, ownership, custody, acquisition, and publication for 
the Ring of Nestor.[9][10] The distinction between the Palace of Nestor and the
separately named ring is maintained because the cited archaeological and 
exhibition records do not identify them as the same object.[10]', 
'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5', 'e6', 'e9', 'e10', 'e11', 
'e12', 'e13', 'e14', 'e15', 'e16', 'e17', 'e18', 'e19', 'e20', 'e21', 'e22', 
'e23', 'e24', 'e25', 'e26', 'e27', 'e34', 'e35', 'e37', 'e38', 'e39', 'e40', 
