"severity": "hard", "report": "# Ring of Nestor\n\n**Ring of Nestor** is a name
used for an Aegean gold ring whose imagery and authenticity have been debated 
in archaeological literature. A 2011 peer-reviewed article states that the ring
was reportedly found in 1924, that Sir Arthur Evans accepted it as authentic, 
and that other scholars judged it a forgery; the same abstract reports later 
renewed claims of authenticity.[5] Theodore Eliopoulos published a 2013 study 
specifically on the ring’s iconography.[1]\n\nThe terminology is not uniform. 
An archival study uses “Minos Ring,” while one of its references uses 
“Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring”; the cited text does not explain whether those 
designations refer to the same object.[8] This naming difference is separate 
from the authenticity disagreement: the 2011 article reports Evans’s 
acceptance, opposing judgments of forgery, and later unnamed claims of 
authenticity, while the archival abstract reports Marinatos’s and Karo’s 
forgery assessment alongside a statement that later evidence suggested 
genuineness.[5][8] The ring is also distinct in the cited publications from the
gold signet rings reported from the Griffin Warrior burial at Pylos, which are 
not identified as the Ring of Nestor.[15]\n\n## Description and 
iconography\n\n## Description and iconography\n\nA 2013 article by Theodore 
Eliopoulos is specifically devoted to the iconography of the “Ring of 
Nestor.”[1] A separate study of Thessaloniki museum inventory MΘ 23748 
describes an unknown-provenance gold signet ring with an elongated, almost 
wedge-shaped bezel, a flat-convex upper surface, a concave reverse, and a 
nearly circular plain hoop.[16] Its intaglio depicts two horned, hoofed 
quadrupeds in a mating scene, although their species cannot be identified 
securely.[16] That study does not equate MΘ 23748 with the Ring of Nestor; the 
physical description therefore remains attached to that inventory 
number.[16]\n\nSeparate Pylos publications describe gold signet rings from the 
Griffin Warrior burial. One displayed summary describes a ring with a seascape,
a shrine flanked by palm trees, and five female figures; the summary does not 
call it the Ring of Nestor.[15] The cited Pylos material therefore 
distinguishes those Pylos rings from the ring discussed in the 2011 Nestor Ring
study.[5][15]\n\nThe terminology presents a specific uncertainty: the cited 
archival study uses “Minos Ring,” while a reference title uses “Pseudo-Minoan 
Nestor Ring,” and the cited text does not explain whether those designations 
denote the same object.[8] Nanno Marinatos, an archaeologist and co-author with
Briana Jackson of the 2011 peer-reviewed study, presents semantic coherence and
Egyptian prototypes as part of that study’s interpretive framework; its 
abstract states that the butterfly motif argues against authenticity.[5] The 
names are therefore reported as source-specific designations rather than 
treated as proven synonyms.[1][8]\n\n## Provenance and publication 
history\n\n## Provenance and publication history\n\nThe documented 
archaeological setting associated with the name Nestor is the Palace of Nestor 
at Ano Englianos in Messenia. Excavations begun in 1939 uncovered the palace, 
and subsequent project publications address the history of investigations in 
Messenia, the palace, and its Linear B documents.[10][9][20] The palace 
excavation continued in annual campaigns from 1952 to 1964, while the palace 
itself was destroyed by fire at the end of the 13th century BCE.[19] These 
facts establish the context of Pylos archaeology, not the findspot or date of 
the Ring of Nestor.[9][10][19]\n\nA 2025 publication describes exhibitions in 
Kalamata, Malibu, and Athens devoted to the wider Kingdom of Pylos, but the 
catalogue description does not identify the Ring of Nestor as an exhibited 
object or provide its ownership and custody history.[10] The cited Pylos 
publications likewise do not provide an object-level discovery or accession 
record for the named ring.[9][10]\n\nThe available publication listings 
therefore do not establish a continuous chronology of discovery, ownership, 
custody, acquisition, and publication for the Ring of Nestor.[9][10] The 
distinction between the Palace of Nestor and the separately named ring is 
maintained because the cited archaeological and exhibition records do not 
identify them as the same object.[10]\n\n## Authenticity debate\n\n## 
Authenticity debate\n\nThe authenticity of the object called the Ring of Nestor
has not been resolved by the supplied sources. Nanno Marinatos, an 
archaeologist, and Briana Jackson report in a peer-reviewed 2011 article that 
the ring was found in 1924, that Sir Arthur Evans accepted it as authentic, and
that many other scholars condemned it as a forgery.[5] The same abstract 
reports renewed claims of authenticity in later scholarship, but it does not 
name the scholars or give the evidence for those claims.[5] These statements 
document a history of competing assessments rather than independently proving 
either authenticity or forgery.\n\nThe 2011 study presents two arguments 
against authenticity. First, it judges the reported circumstances of discovery 
suspicious. Second, it states that the use of the butterfly motif argues 
against authenticity.[5] The supplied abstract does not specify the discovery 
circumstances, provide the comparative corpus, or reproduce the detailed 
iconographic analysis, so the strength of those arguments cannot be assessed 
here beyond the authors’ stated conclusion.\n\nThe same study’s framework gives
a different kind of argument: Marinatos and Jackson state that semantic 
coherence depends on Egyptian prototypes.[5] This is an interpretive criterion,
not a materials test or excavation record. The supplied material contains no 
authenticated scientific examination of the Ring of Nestor’s alloy, 
manufacture, tool marks, or engraving sequence. An automated summary of 
Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 article reports that a connection between incurved 
altars and the ring’s depiction supports authenticity, but the underlying 
article text and argument are not available in the supplied record.[1] That 
statement is consequently weaker evidence than the peer-reviewed article’s 
explicit abstract-level conclusions.\n\nAn archival study using the name “Minos
Ring” adds a further, but not fully transferable, disagreement. Its abstract 
states that Spyridon Marinatos and Georg Karo regarded that ring as a forgery, 
while new evidence in an unavailable appendix was said to suggest that it was 
genuine.[8] The cited bibliography uses “Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring,” whereas 
the study’s title and abstract use “Minos Ring”; the supplied material does not
establish that these names refer to the same object.[8] The authenticity 
positions can therefore be reported as part of the documented debate, but the 
available evidence does not justify combining them with other Pylos-related 
rings.[8]\n\n## References\n\n[1] SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE 
‘RING OF NESTOR’ 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_R
ING_OF_NESTOR_\n\n[5] The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian 
Iconography |\nJournal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections 
https://journals.librarypublishing.arizona.edu/jaei/article/id/960\n\n[8] On 
the Authenticity of the “Minos Ring”: Evidence from Spyridon Marinatos’ 
Archives 
https://www.academia.edu/15668908/On_the_Authenticity_of_the_Minos_Ring_Evidenc
